+Machine-learning applications in groundwater-quality assessment often report high predictive performance without demonstrating control of target leakage, spatial dependence, class imbalance and small-sample uncertainty. This study developed a leakage-controlled virtual-screening framework to prioritise wells at elevated probability of exceeding precautionary drinking-water screening thresholds in the semi-arid Pru Basin, Ghana, using 81 georeferenced groundwater samples. Six groundwater-quality variables (Na, Cl, TDS, B, F and NO₃) were converted into binary risk-alert labels at study-defined precautionary thresholds; NO₃ was excluded from modelling because only two wells exceeded its screening threshold. Target-derived predictors were removed before modelling, preprocessing and hyperparameter tuning were contained within training folds, and operational probability cutoffs were selected inside each outer cross-validation fold from internal-validation predictions only, so that no held-out information entered any modelling decision. Na, Cl and TDS were the most screenable targets: pooled out-of-fold PR-AUC reached 0.978 for Cl and 0.992 for TDS with field-only predictors, and spatial-group cross-validation retained PR-AUC values of 0.977 (Cl) and 0.964 (TDS, full tier) for the salinity-linked targets. For TDS, an EC-strict model that excluded the conductivity proxy retained PR-AUC = 0.976 (well-level bootstrap 95% CI 1.000–1.000) under stratified validation and 0.923 under spatial validation. B showed moderate, chemistry-dependent performance (0.813 best), whereas F was more spatially fragile (0.636 stratified vs 0.490 spatial). Removing coordinates did not erase discrimination for any modelled target, and relabelling at current WHO guideline values left the ranking of screenability unchanged wherever sufficient positives remained. Well-level decision metrics (median out-of-fold probability per well, each well counted once) gave interpretable false-positive/false-negative burdens for confirmatory-testing prioritisation, and Brier scores were benchmarked against prevalence-only baselines (Brier skill 0.770 for the EC-strict TDS model). We recommend the EC-strict TDS model and the full-chemistry random-forest model for B as the primary screening tools, with target-specific cutoffs applied as described in Table 4. The framework supports conservative well prioritisation for confirmatory testing in data-limited groundwater surveillance; it does not replace laboratory confirmation or regulatory monitoring.
+
+**Keywords:** confirmatory-testing prioritisation; groundwater screening; path-based feature attribution; precision–recall analysis; spatial validation; well prioritisation
 
