-Groundwater is a critical source of drinking water, domestic supply and irrigation water in semi-arid and rural regions where surface-water resources are seasonally constrained or unreliable. Its quality is controlled by coupled geogenic and anthropogenic processes, including lithology, mineral weathering, ion exchange, salinisation, agricultural return flow and localised surface-derived inputs (Sunkari et al., 2021). In the semi-arid Pru Basin, groundwater occurs within a hydrogeologically constrained Voltaian setting where shallow fractured aquifers, ferricrete cover, argillaceous and arenaceous rocks, low primary porosity and variable secondary permeability influence groundwater occurrence and chemistry (Sunkari et al., 2021). Regular groundwater-quality assessment is therefore essential for public-health protection and sustainable water-resource management, particularly where laboratory monitoring resources are limited. The World Health Organization drinking-water guideline framework provides an authoritative basis for risk management and national standard setting, but operational surveillance also requires efficient tools that can prioritise wells for confirmatory testing rather than replace laboratory analysis (World Health Organization, 2022).
-
-Machine-learning and data-driven screening approaches are increasingly used to support environmental monitoring, water-quality classification and contaminant-risk prioritisation. Algorithms such as logistic regression, random forests and gradient-boosted trees provide complementary modelling capacities: logistic regression supports interpretable linear screening, random forests provide ensemble-based nonlinear classification, and gradient boosting captures additive nonlinear structure through sequential learning (Breiman, 2001; Friedman, 2001). However, groundwater datasets are often small, spatially clustered and class imbalanced. These conditions make performance estimates vulnerable to methodological bias when target-derived predictors, spatially neighbouring wells or model-selection information enter validation folds. Precision--recall analysis is particularly relevant where positive exceedance cases are sparse, because PR-AUC focuses directly on positive-class retrieval rather than being dominated by the majority class (Davis & Goadrich, 2006). Similarly, nested cross-validation is required when tuning is performed, because tuning and estimating performance in the same loop can bias error estimates (Varma & Simon, 2006).
-
-The central problem addressed in this study is that high reported predictive performance in groundwater machine learning may not be operationally reliable unless leakage, spatial dependence, fold-level uncertainty and threshold selection are explicitly controlled. In the Pru Basin dataset, the modelling challenge is sharpened by the small sample size of 81 wells, unequal target prevalence and chemically interdependent predictors. Sodium, chloride and TDS are expected to be more screenable because they are linked to coherent salinity and mineralisation gradients, whereas fluoride, boron and nitrate may be influenced by more localised geochemical, lithological or anthropogenic controls (Sunkari et al., 2021). Therefore, the relevant question is not whether a single model can classify all groundwater-quality targets uniformly, but which targets can be screened with sufficient reliability under conservative validation.
-
-This study develops a leakage-controlled, spatially conservative virtual-screening framework for groundwater exceedance prioritisation in a small semi-arid basin dataset. The workflow converts raw groundwater chemistry and coordinates into binary precautionary screening labels, applies target-wise leakage filtering, compares predictor tiers, trains validated classifiers and generates interpretable screening-priority outputs. Target thresholds are treated as predefined precautionary screening cut-offs used to represent early-warning risk-alert levels rather than regulatory exceedance decisions. This precautionary design supports surveillance planning while preserving the need for confirmatory laboratory testing.
-
-The novelty of the framework lies in four integrated safeguards. First, target-specific leakage control removes direct target variables, target-derived ratios and circular proxies before model training. Second, tiered field-to-chemistry modelling evaluates whether low-cost field variables can retain useful screening performance relative to reduced and full hydrochemical predictor sets. Third, repeated stratified nested cross-validation is paired with spatial-group validation, allowing random-resampling performance to be compared with geographically conservative transferability estimates. Fourth, SHAP-based model interpretation and GIS-ready screening-priority maps translate validated probabilities into decision-support outputs without presenting the maps as definitive contamination distributions (Lundberg & Lee, 2017).
-
-The aim is to develop and evaluate a leakage-controlled, small-sample virtual-screening framework for identifying wells at elevated risk of exceeding precautionary drinking-water thresholds. The objectives are to assess target-specific screenability using field and reduced-chemistry variables, compare repeated stratified nested cross-validation with spatial validation, evaluate reduced-variable screening for well prioritisation and identify predictors influencing model behaviour using explainable machine-learning diagnostics. The study is significant because it provides a reproducible approach for groundwater surveillance in data-limited semi-arid environments while explicitly avoiding claims that machine-learning screening replaces laboratory confirmation.
+Groundwater is a critical source of drinking water, domestic supply and irrigation water in semi-arid and rural regions where surface-water resources are seasonally constrained or unreliable. Its quality is controlled by coupled geogenic and anthropogenic processes, including lithology, mineral weathering, ion exchange, salinisation, agricultural return flow and localised surface-derived inputs (Sunkari et al., 2021). In the semi-arid Pru Basin, groundwater occurs within a hydrogeologically constrained Voltaian setting where shallow fractured aquifers, ferricrete cover, argillaceous and arenaceous rocks, low primary porosity and variable secondary permeability influence groundwater occurrence and chemistry (Sunkari et al., 2021). Regular groundwater-quality assessment is essential for public-health protection, particularly where laboratory monitoring resources are limited; the WHO drinking-water guideline framework provides the authoritative basis for risk management and national standard setting, but operational surveillance also requires efficient tools that prioritise wells for confirmatory testing rather than replace laboratory analysis (World Health Organization, 2022). Recent assessments in other data-limited regions reinforce this need: drinking-water quality and the associated human health risks of potentially toxic elements remain unevenly characterised across many semi-arid and rural settings (Abdipour et al., 2026), and land-use decisions can measurably alter groundwater quality at regional scales (Zhan et al., 2025).
+
+Machine-learning and data-driven screening approaches are increasingly used to support environmental monitoring, water-quality classification and contaminant-risk prioritisation. Algorithms such as logistic regression, random forests and gradient-boosted trees provide complementary modelling capacities: logistic regression supports interpretable linear screening, random forests provide ensemble-based nonlinear classification, and gradient boosting captures additive nonlinear structure through sequential learning (Breiman, 2001; Friedman, 2001). However, groundwater datasets are often small, spatially clustered and class imbalanced. These conditions make performance estimates vulnerable to methodological bias when target-derived predictors, spatially neighbouring wells or model-selection information enter validation folds (Roberts et al., 2017; Varma & Simon, 2006). Precision–recall analysis is particularly relevant where positive exceedance cases are sparse, because PR-AUC focuses directly on positive-class retrieval rather than being dominated by the majority class (Davis & Goadrich, 2006). Recent methodological work in adjacent fields illustrates the same priorities that guide this study: uncertainty quantification in groundwater-table deep learning (Abbaszadeh Shahri et al., 2022), physics-informed deep learning for subsurface exchange imaging (Yin et al., 2025), and data-driven modelling of fractured-reservoir systems (Abbasi & Madani, 2026) all emphasise calibrated, uncertainty-aware prediction under data limitation rather than raw discrimination alone.
+
+The central problem addressed here is that high reported predictive performance in groundwater machine learning may not be operationally reliable unless leakage, spatial dependence, fold-level uncertainty and threshold selection are explicitly controlled. In the Pru Basin dataset, the modelling challenge is sharpened by a small sample size (81 wells), unequal target prevalence and chemically interdependent predictors. Sodium, chloride and TDS are expected to be more screenable because they are linked to coherent salinity and mineralisation gradients, whereas fluoride, boron and nitrate may be influenced by more localised geochemical, lithological or anthropogenic controls (Sunkari et al., 2021). The relevant question is therefore not whether a single model can classify all groundwater-quality targets uniformly, but which targets can be screened with sufficient reliability under conservative validation, and with what false-positive/false-negative burden at an explicitly defined operating point.
+
+The novelty of this framework lies in an integrated validation architecture rather than in any single algorithm. First, target-specific leakage control removes direct target variables, target-derived ratios and circular proxies before model training. Second, tiered field-to-chemistry modelling evaluates whether low-cost field variables retain useful screening performance relative to reduced and full hydrochemical predictor sets. Third, repeated stratified nested cross-validation is paired with spatial-group validation in which all spatial folds — including single-class folds — are reported transparently and pooled spatial performance is computed across all unique held-out wells. Fourth, operational probability cutoffs are selected inside each outer fold from internal-validation predictions (fully nested threshold selection), eliminating the optimistic bias of post-hoc pooled cutoff selection. Fifth, well-level consolidated out-of-fold probabilities provide unique-well decision metrics for confirmatory-testing prioritisation, and Brier scores are benchmarked against prevalence-only baselines. Finally, path-based (Saabas-style) feature attribution and GIS-ready screening-priority maps translate validated probabilities into decision-support outputs without presenting them as definitive contamination distributions. To our knowledge, no previous groundwater-screening study combines all of these safeguards in a single small-sample framework; earlier work in this basin used deterministic hydrochemical classification without predictive validation (Sunkari et al., 2021), and recent ensemble studies of Ghanaian groundwater quality identified the absence of spatial cross-validation as an explicit limitation (Ansah-Narh et al., 2026).
+
+The aim of this study was to develop and evaluate a leakage-controlled, small-sample virtual-screening framework for identifying wells at elevated risk of exceeding precautionary drinking-water thresholds. The objectives were to (i) assess target-specific screenability under field, reduced-chemistry and full-chemistry predictor tiers; (ii) compare repeated stratified nested cross-validation with spatial-group validation, including a transparent treatment of single-class spatial folds; (iii) quantify the contribution of explicit geographic coordinates through a coordinate-ablation design; (iv) evaluate operational decision thresholds through fully nested cutoff selection and well-level decision metrics, including sensitivity to the recall-weighting parameter of the F-score; and (v) test sensitivity to concentration-threshold definition, below-detection-limit handling and the EC conductivity proxy. The study is significant because it provides a reproducible approach for groundwater surveillance in data-limited semi-arid environments while explicitly avoiding claims that machine-learning screening replaces laboratory confirmation.
+
 
