+
+Decision support is provided at two levels. First, target-specific operating points: the fully nested sensitivity-first cutoffs described in Section 2.8, aggregated across folds. Second, well-level decision metrics: for each well, the median of its repeated out-of-fold probabilities (each well contributing exactly once), followed by unique-well true-positive (TP), true-negative (TN), false-positive (FP) and false-negative (FN) counts at a well-level cutoff selected with the same sensitivity-first rule (Table 4). Pooled prediction-instance counts, in which a well can contribute several times across repeats, are retained only as explicitly labelled prediction-instance counts and are not used for principal decision support. Screening-priority classes were defined on the well-level median out-of-fold probability of the best canonical (non-dummy, non-sensitivity) model for each target, using pre-specified probability bands: Low (< 0.25), Moderate (0.25–0.50), High (0.50–0.75) and Very high (≥ 0.75). These bands are screening-priority statements for confirmatory testing, not definitive contamination classifications.
